 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Lookup is $O(1)$ on average for multiplicative hashing [S5, unofficial]. Construction of perfect hash tables is $O(M^{3/2})$ in the worst case for 2D matrices, but efficient for static sparse data [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the hash table size $T$, not the volume resolution. This allows high-resolution rendering with low memory usage [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Significantly faster than tree-based structures for dense queries due to cache locality and lack of pointer chasing [S1][S5, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Low memory footprint, fast lookup, simple implementation [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Hash collisions can degrade performance and cause visual artifacts (feature interference) [S1, S8]. Inverse mapping is expensive [S8].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Lookup is $O(1)$ on average for multiplicative hashing [S5, unofficial]. Construction of perfect hash tables is $O(M^{3/2})$ in the worst case for 2D matrices [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the hash table size $T$, not the volume resolution [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Low memory footprint and fast lookup [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Hash collisions can cause feature interference, requiring mitigation strategies like hash fusion or inverse mapping [S8, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Hash Grids were introduced in 1977 by Aho and Ullman as a compaction scheme for transition diagrams of deterministic finite automata [S1]. The technique was later adapted for ray tracing by Lagae and Dutre (2008) to compress sparse 3D matrices using row displacement compression [S2, S3, unofficial]. In recent years, multiplicative hashing has become standard in neural radiance fields (e.g., Instant NGP) for efficient feature storage [S5, unofficial][S8].
